{"ClientSettings":{"Pn":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"P"},"Sc":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"H"},"Qz":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"T"},"Ap":true,"Mute":true,"Lad":"2026-11-27T00:00:00Z","Iotd":0,"Gwb":0,"Tns":0,"Dft":null,"Mvs":0,"Flt":0,"Imp":1,"Tobn":0},"MediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"With their colorful plumage and distinctive gobble, adult male turkeys, known as toms or gobblers, are a familiar sight of the season. Toms, like those pictured here, fan their tail feathers and show off their rich coloring to attract hens. These native birds can be found in the wild across much of North America, often in forest and woodland habitats. Turkeys have also become closely tied to Thanksgiving traditions, serving as a familiar centerpiece of a holiday feast that, for many, is less about what's on the table and more about gathering with family and friends. In today's image, two toms take a Thanksgiving stroll and put their finest feathers forward in the warm glow of autumn.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.WildTom_EN-US1069249609_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.WildTom_EN-US1069249609_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Turkey twins","Title":"Two wild tom turkeys","Copyright":"© davidsdodd/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"Historical accounts of the 1621 harvest feast don't specifically mention turkey as a menu item, referring instead to \"wild fowl,\" which could have been duck or geese.","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261126_WildTom\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Thanksgiving&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261126_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261126_WildTom"},"Ssd":"20261126","Name":"WildTom","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"1069249609","FullDateString":"Nov 26, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Snow transforms Bryce Canyon National Park, blanketing the landscape in white while its fiery orange-red hoodoos rise above the snowy terrain. Seen from Sunset Point, one of the park's most famous overlooks, these fantastical shapes are part of the Claron Formation, whose limestone was deposited about 50 million years ago and later shaped by weathering and erosion. Because much of the park rises above 8,000 feet, snow can arrive as early as October. In today's image, snow clings to the canyon's ridges and spires while mist drifts across the cliffs, creating an iconic winter scene in the American Southwest.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SunsetPoint_EN-US0827880440_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SunsetPoint_EN-US0827880440_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"A southwestern snowfall","Title":"Sunset Point, Bryce Canyon National Park, Utah","Copyright":"© Colin D. Young/Alamy","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/37.623535,-112.167279/6?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=37.623535,-112.167279;S9;Sunset Point, Bryce Canyon National Park, Utah&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261125_SunsetPoint\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Bryce+Canyon+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261125_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261125_SunsetPoint"},"Ssd":"20261125","Name":"SunsetPoint","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"0827880440","FullDateString":"Nov 25, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"As Britain's only native squirrel species, red squirrels thrive in Scotland's Cairngorms National Park. An important refuge, the park offers ancient Caledonian pine forests and newer conifer woodlands, with plentiful habitat and food for squirrels foraging for Scots pine seeds, nuts, and other tasty morsels year-round. In today's image, a red squirrel is perched amid warm orange and yellow leaves, looking right at home as it ponders its next move in the Scottish Highlands.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.CairngormsSquirrel_EN-US7053432564_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.CairngormsSquirrel_EN-US7053432564_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Autumnal camouflage","Title":"Red squirrel in Cairngorms National Park, Highlands, Scotland","Copyright":"© Scotland: The Big Picture/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/57.0833,-3.6667/8?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=57.0833,-3.6667;S9;Cairngorms National Park, Highlands, Scotland&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261124_CairngormsSquirrel\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Eurasian+red+squirrel&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261124_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261124_CairngormsSquirrel"},"Ssd":"20261124","Name":"CairngormsSquirrel","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"7053432564","FullDateString":"Nov 24, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Today's image shows the gradual coiling of a young fiddlehead's tip, echoing the geometric patterns seen throughout nature. In math, the Fibonacci sequence follows a similar progression, each number formed by adding the two before it: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, and so on. Today is Fibonacci Day, chosen for the numbers of the date (11/23) to honor the sequence. Eventually the young fern frond shown here will unfurl as it grows, but elsewhere in the plant world, the sequence will continue to appear in the spiraling arrangements of sunflower heads, pinecones, succulents, and pineapples.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.FibonacciFern_EN-US6307771770_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.FibonacciFern_EN-US6307771770_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Geometry in nature","Title":"Fiddlehead fern","Copyright":"© Reza Saputra/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"The Fibonacci sequence was popularized through a 13th-century math problem about multiplying rabbits.","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261123_FibonacciFern\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=fibonacci+day&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261123_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261123_FibonacciFern"},"Ssd":"20261123","Name":"FibonacciFern","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"6307771770","FullDateString":"Nov 23, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Tucked into the cliffs of northern Spain's rugged coastline, the 16th-century Hermitage of Santa Justa seen in today's image overlooks the crashing waves of the Cantabrian Sea. Built partly into the rock and associated with relics of Saints Justa and Rufina, the small chapel eventually became a pilgrimage site, its sheltered stone walls creating a remarkable contrast with the powerful waves and rocky cliffs surrounding it.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.JustaHermitage_EN-US0130271816_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.JustaHermitage_EN-US0130271816_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Shelter among the waves","Title":"The chapel and hermitage of Santa Justa in Cantabria, Spain","Copyright":"© Tramont_ana/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/43.42595,-4.09762/9?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=43.42595,-4.09762;S9;Santa Justa, Cantabria&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261122_JustaHermitage\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Ermita+Santa+Justa+chapel&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261122_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261122_JustaHermitage"},"Ssd":"20261122","Name":"JustaHermitage","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"0130271816","FullDateString":"Nov 22, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Bathed in a sun-kissed glow, Nice is a patchwork of terracotta rooftops, historic churches, and pastel façades along the French Riviera, as seen in today's image. Founded by ancient Greeks and long connected to the Italian royal House of Savoy, Nice officially became part of France in 1860. Today, that layered past remains visible throughout the coastal city in the south of France, where old-world architecture meets the blue Mediterranean waters and sunlit hills of the Côte d'Azur.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.RedNice_EN-US9978351403_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.RedNice_EN-US9978351403_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Terracotta dreams","Title":"Nice, Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, France","Copyright":"© Siripong Kaewla-iad/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/43.70094,7.26839/5?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=43.70094,7.26839;S9;Nice, France&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261121_RedNice\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Nice+France&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261121_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261121_RedNice"},"Ssd":"20261121","Name":"RedNice","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"9978351403","FullDateString":"Nov 21, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Off the coast of Kvaløya in Troms, Northern Norway, humpback whales move through the cold Arctic waters in search of a proper feast. They visit this region during the winter months, following migrating herring into the fjords. Humpbacks are famous for bubble-net feeding, a remarkable hunting technique in which whales blow rings of bubbles to corral fish before lunging upward, open-mouthed, to catch their prey. In today's image, a humpback slips beneath the icy waves under a glowing pastel sky, capturing a moment of tranquility in Norway's wild north.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.Megaptera_EN-US1728719939_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.Megaptera_EN-US1728719939_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Feasting in the fjords","Title":"Diving humpback whale, Kvaloya, Troms, Northern Norway","Copyright":"© Espen Bergersen/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/69.62389,18.53103/7?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=69.62389,18.53103;S9;Kvaloya, Troms, Norway&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261120_Megaptera\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=humpback+whales&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261120_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261120_Megaptera"},"Ssd":"20261120","Name":"Megaptera","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"1728719939","FullDateString":"Nov 20, 2026"}],"PreloadMediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Deep within Ranthambore National Park, Indian banyan trees take root. Let the other trees grow from the ground up; banyan seeds sprout on branches, buildings, or ruins, dropped by passing birds. Once there, their aerial roots descend to the ground, slowly thickening into branch-supporting trunks. This allows the fig trees to spread over huge areas, providing fruit and shelter for wildlife, and welcome shade for people.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.FicasBenghalensis_EN-US7128870149_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.FicasBenghalensis_EN-US7128870149_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Rooted in history","Title":"Indian banyan tree on arch ruin, Ranthambore National Park, India","Copyright":"© Vincent Grafhorst/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/27.304257,76.582332/6?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=27.304257,76.582332;S9;Ranthambore National Park&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261203_FicasBenghalensis\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Ranthambore+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261203_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261203_FicasBenghalensis"},"Ssd":"20261203","Name":"FicasBenghalensis","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"7128870149","FullDateString":"Dec 3, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"The fortress city of Valletta towers above the coast of Malta, surrounded by imposing walls rising out of the Mediterranean Sea. Founded in 1566, a year after the island country came under siege from the Ottoman Empire, it was built by the ruling knights of the Order of St. John to ward off future invasions.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ValettaSunrise_EN-US6993641480_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ValettaSunrise_EN-US6993641480_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Dawn over Valletta","Title":"The historic city of Valletta, Malta","Copyright":"© RudyBalasko/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/35.898866,14.512414/8?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=35.898866,14.512414;S9;Valletta, Malta&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261202_ValettaSunrise\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=valletta+malta&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261202_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261202_ValettaSunrise"},"Ssd":"20261202","Name":"ValettaSunrise","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"6993641480","FullDateString":"Dec 2, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Who better to wish us a happy Antarctica Day than this waddle of emperor penguins, one of the frozen continent's most iconic species. While humans here are restricted to scientific research stations and expeditions, emperor penguins have learned to adapt to Antarctica's inhospitable climate. The emperors' dense feathers and fat reserves help to keep them warm. And this sociable bunch know how to share—they huddle together in groups, shuffling around so each gets a turn in the center, where it's warmest.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.EmperorAntarctica_EN-US6872270978_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.EmperorAntarctica_EN-US6872270978_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Home of the emperors","Title":"Emperor penguins, Queen Maud Land, Antarctica","Copyright":"© Stefan Christmann/Nature Picture Library","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/-78.984829,75.491598/1?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=-78.984829,75.491598;S9;Antarctica&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261201_EmperorAntarctica\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Antarctica+Day&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261201_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261201_EmperorAntarctica"},"Ssd":"20261201","Name":"EmperorAntarctica","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"6872270978","FullDateString":"Dec 1, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Happy St. Andrew's Day! To celebrate the Scottish holiday, we're looking out over one of Scotland's most otherworldly landscapes, formed by ancient landslides on the Isle of Skye. Millions of years ago, layers of heavy lava settled here on top of soft clay and rock, before gradually sliding down toward the sea. It left steep slopes, hidden plateaus, and jagged rock formations along the Trotternish Peninsula, which forms Skye's northernmost tip.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.QuiraingSkye_EN-US6751305839_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.QuiraingSkye_EN-US6751305839_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Skye's sliding landscape","Title":"The Quiraing, Trotternish, Isle of Skye, Scotland","Copyright":"© Matt Anderson Photography/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/57.655679,-6.277862/8?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=57.655679,-6.277862;S9;The Quiraing, Skye, Scotland&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261130_QuiraingSkye\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=St+Andrew%27s+Day&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261130_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261130_QuiraingSkye"},"Ssd":"20261130","Name":"QuiraingSkye","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"6751305839","FullDateString":"Nov 30, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Lit by streetlights as it crosses this narrow stretch of the Norwegian Sea, Sommarøy Bridge is the only road onto the small fishing island that shares its name. From a distance, with its white sand beaches and turquoise waters, you might think you were somewhere tropical. But Sommarøy sits north of the Arctic Circle, and those are icy waters you're crossing.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SommaroyBridge_EN-US6643887924_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SommaroyBridge_EN-US6643887924_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Skimming the sea","Title":"Sommarøy Bridge from above, Tromso, Norway","Copyright":"© Roberto Moiola/Sysaworld/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/69.623346,18.045599/7?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=69.623346,18.045599;S9;Sommarøy Bridge, Norway&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261129_SommaroyBridge\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Sommar%c3%b8y+Norway&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261129_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261129_SommaroyBridge"},"Ssd":"20261129","Name":"SommaroyBridge","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"6643887924","FullDateString":"Nov 29, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"This autumnal view from Letná Park looks out over Prague, which grew up along the banks of the Vltava River in Czechia. In today's image, the river winds its way through the historic Old Town and Malá Strana districts, beneath Mánes Bridge in the foreground. Many beautiful bridges now span the Vltava, but for centuries there was only one, Charles Bridge, pictured second from the bottom. Its first stones were laid in 1357 during the reign of Charles IV and until 1841, it was the only way to cross the river on foot. Today its stone arches, gothic towers, and statues lining the bridge draw large crowds, so tourists are advised to get there by dawn for the best photographs. Or they could walk on to the next crossing, Legion Bridge, which is less crowded and offers beautiful views of the National Theatre, Prague Castle, and its busier neighbor.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.VltavaPrague_EN-US6068116977_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.VltavaPrague_EN-US6068116977_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Bridges through time","Title":"Old town and Vltava River, Prague, Czechia","Copyright":"© Lara_Uhryn/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/50.089959,14.412867/6?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=50.089959,14.412867;S9;Mánes Bridge, Prague&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261128_VltavaPrague\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Prague+bridges&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261128_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261128_VltavaPrague"},"Ssd":"20261128","Name":"VltavaPrague","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"6068116977","FullDateString":"Nov 28, 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Hidden within an ancient sandstone landscape, Face Canyon is one of 96 canyons stretching out from Lake Powell in southern Utah. Many are far from roads and would be hard to reach if they hadn't been flooded by the lake, created in 1963 during the construction of the Glen Canyon Dam. The lake's waters back up about 186 miles, along creeks and canyons which you can explore by boat, kayak, or personal watercraft.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.FaceCanyon_EN-US6254374163_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.FaceCanyon_EN-US6254374163_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Ribbons of gray and gold","Title":"Butte at sunset, Face Canyon, Lake Powell, Utah","Copyright":"© Jeff Foott/Minden","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"//platform.bing.com/geo/REST/v1/Imagery/Map/RoadVibrant/37.002262,-111.24176/5?key=AsSOKo7OOz5VAtfAj0rjgaXlhCrCZI6PGbLj7GCH8IW2HUalyg4BVhqA0z77PRCj&c=en-US&od=2&shading=flat&pp=37.002262,-111.24176;S9;Face Canyon, Utah&st=pp|v:false;lv:false_trs|v:false;lv:false&ml=Basemap,Landmarks&logo=no&mapSize=386,434&da=ro","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261127_FaceCanyon\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Lake+Powell&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261127_0800\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261127_FaceCanyon"},"Ssd":"20261127","Name":"FaceCanyon","Market":"EN-US","Hash":"6254374163","FullDateString":"Nov 27, 2026"}],"LocStrings":{"LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_IOTD":"Image of the day","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_IOTD_LEARN_MORE":"Learn more","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_FACT":"Quick fact:","LOC_HOMEPAGE_GALLERY_CARD_TITLE":"Recent Homepage images","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_GALLERY_IMAGE_ARIALABEL":"Homepage image for {0}","LOC_HOMEPAGE_ON_THIS_DAY_TITLE":"On this day in history","LOC_HOMEPAGE_ON_THIS_DAY_IMAGE_ARIALABEL":"On this day in {0}, {1}","LOC_HOMEPAGE_SHARE_TOOLTIP":"Share today's Bing homepage","LOC_HOMEPAGE_WALLPAPE